264		For POVMs $\{A^x_a\}$ and $\{B^x_a\}$, the following hold.
265		\begin{enumerate}
266			\item If $A^x_a \ot \Id_B \simeq_\delta \Id_A \ot B^x_a$, then $A^x_a \ot \Id_B \approx_\delta \Id_A \ot B^x_a$.
267			\item If $A^x_a \ot \Id_B \approx_\delta \Id_A \ot B^x_a$ \emph{and} $\{A^x_a\}$ and $\{B^x_a\}$ are projective measurements, then $A^x_a \ot \Id_B \simeq_\delta \Id_A \ot B^x_a$.
268		\end{enumerate}
269	\end{lemma}
270	\begin{proof}
271		\leanok
272		\uses{lem:distance-consistency-term,
273			lem:distance-point-distance-le-defect,
274			lem:distance-projective-point-distance, lem:distance-nonnegative}
275		Stated in the source without proof; this is Fact 4.13 of \cite{Natarajan2018NEEXP}. For the first item, expand each term $\lVert (A^x_a \ot \Id - \Id \ot B^x_a)\ket{\psi} \rVert^2$ and use $ (A^x_a)^2 \le A^x_a$, $(B^x_a)^2 \le B^x_a$ together with completeness of the two POVMs: summing over $a$ and averaging over $x$ bounds the total by at most twice the off-diagonal mass, which is $O(\delta)$ by hypothesis. When both families are projective the two operator inequalities hold with equality, so the expansion equals exactly twice the off-diagonal mass; reading this identity in the other direction gives the second item.
276	\end{proof}
